ike changes in the gut microbiome and  that in turn affects the health of the  astronauts because we know that gut  microbiota is  connected with a lot of physiological  aspects of  a person like the gut and brain access  then there's risk of cancer that  reproducts reproductive system issues  neurodegenerative diseases  immune response changes metabolism  problems and  many other problems which we are not yet  aware of  so standing at that point i  tried to locate some data sets that are  available freely  and publicly to everybody for a five  other studies so there are numerous  projects  and to be honest very few associated  papers  i have made and collected a few of them  and thought of some  possible projects like this one like the  zebrafish  transcriptome under simulated  microgravity this was one study done in  association with nasa  then there was another data available  with adobe  system took to space  in blue origins new shepherd and virgin  galactic's  spacecrafts so to study what happened to  these  plant samples under the space flight  conditions that is exposed to radiation  microgravity hypergravity  low pressure oxidative stresses and  everything so that would be something  interesting  then there's hepatic stellates cells  under various  types of gravity like 5g 1g simulated  microgravity this particular  data was generated at indian institute  of technology madras  so this is the mrna expression of dld  and mlt  human cancer cell lines under  microgravity and they reported some  transcriptomic changes  again expression level changes  differential gene expressions and stuffs  and also there's one interesting study  which was done using the gut microbiota  and there the scientists found  very interesting patterns in the changes  in the gut microbiome  then they could actually differentiate  the difference  of this microbiota change pattern from a  radiation induced  change and a microgravity induced change  so  the authors suggest that there can be  very  significant patterns which we can notice  uh which the changes which are caused by  radiation and the ones that are caused  by microgravity  so these are some of the data sets that  i have collected and  one project that i have actually started  to  look at the data and hover around is  this one the zebrafish transcriptome  under simulated microgravity  so let's take a look at that this is the  particular  paper associated with it and they  actually studied this  positive feedback loop mechanism which  is actually a virus  induced immune response it involves trim  25  and rig 1 pathway and this  is actually done with the help of  interferon type 1 interferon 1 is very  important in this particular feedback  loop  and these zebrafish embryos were  cultured in you know standard gravity  and rotatory cell vessel which  actually mimic this microgravity  condition and then they were harvested  and their rna profiling was done and  stuffs  their survival rate was also looked at  and  there were two different types of  samples one sample was  which had no treatment and the other one  had the poly ic  vector treatment which is actually a  virus mimic and it  attaches itself with the two leg  receptor three  and it generates an immune response so  these are the two  conditions so in all we have four  conditions  so what they actually found was  the trim 25 which is a rig one regulator  and an interferon  stimulated gene actually had  a down regulation so this  is a very important candidate of this  positive feedback loop  uh that is triggered to antiviral immune  responses but due to the  microgravity simulated microgravity  conditions this was reduced  and since this was reduced the entire  feedback loop  was kind of hampered and that made  the cells more susceptible to the viral  infection  interestingly it was also found that  upon rescuing the  trim25 gene and not letting the  expression  fall the again the  feedback loop was unharmed and it was  saved from being hampered  so the cells maintained their antiviral  immune response capability so the  authors discussed there that this trim25  is a potential therapeutic target for  maintaining the  immune homeostasis under microgravity  for astronauts in the future and  till date there's no molecule agonist  identified  which can actually bind to the trim 25  protein  and they suggest that maybe admin  associated virus mediated crispr  activation that is a crispr a system  using a single guide rna could be a  potential solution which uh instead of  any small molecule drug or small  molecule agonist we can  actually use a crispr activation system  which can bind itself and activate the  expression level of trim 25  and thus you know keeping the workflow  going  so there's another interesting question  that comes up  which is how does microgravity affect  and why does these changes take place in  the first place so  again there are a few very interesting  hypothesis  and speculations like there are  three to five papers i guess in all  which suggest  that stimulated simulated microgravity  substantially affects the epigenetic  modifications  so to be precise they uh show the  scientists have observed epigenetic  changes in myogenic differentiation  process  embryonic development and t lymphocyte  regulation  also certain epigenetic processes like  dna methyl transfer s3 alpha  has been observed to be down regulated  in microgravity  and apart from that dna hypomethylation  h3 hypo acetylation and  certain mutational changes have been  observed  so the authors suggest that maybe this  down regulation  is a result of epigenetic changes  which they did not explore and they said  that it would be interesting to be  to see in the future so this is a data  set that we have as you can see there  are this  normal gravity samples three normal  gravity  and treated three simulated microgravity  three and untreated and  treated three so we have 12 samples in  all and  so this was a brief um analysis of the  data set  i did some pca analysis so the first  pc one versus pc2 has a  pretty strange uh variance 90 and three  but we can see that the normal gravity  both treated and untreated are forming  one particular cluster whereas simulated  microgravity treated  versus stimulated microgravity untreated  is not  forming a clear cluster and  upon doing a pc1 versus pc3 we see the  simulated microgravity ones are  clustering together whereas the non  microgravity normal gravity are far  apart and  pc2 versus pc3 gives a little variance  so they are all  not clustering together they are all  apart  there was one pathway enrichment  analysis which i did and  we can see there are four clusters and  especially in this one which is cluster  c we can see a strong  uh differential gene expression that is  the normal gravity  all the ones are upregulated and these  are down regulated so  i made a list so we can see some pretty  interesting  pathways like embryo development tissue  development  brain development and so on there's a  lot  which we can look into i also did a very  quick differential gene expression  and you can see these numbers  and this is the venn diagram also and  here i have uh  made a little point like a little chart  of  the down regulated pathways and  the up regulated pathways so again we  can see some interesting things like  protein transport  reproduction endoplasmic reticulum  stress  trna processing have been down regulated  am i transport  and under upper regulation we can see  gene expression rna metabolism nucleic  acid metabolism empire development and  so on  so such a exploratory  analysis kind of shows that yeah there  can be interesting  things to look into as well uh further  so what i feel  and what i want to pass on as a  concluding message is there are many  pathways  which are affected by a single space  flight  and space flight means all effects  combined we are only studying  microgravity in this one but  there are many other things which can  affect  and these studies are  really important if we actually want to  settle on any other planet  or explore them for a long duration like  for two years and stuff  because otherwise we are actually  risking the  people those who are going on this  particular mission because we don't  really know what's going to happen to  them  also such studies might help us explore  and understand some  pathways which we did not know about so  far or  to understand the already known pathway  is better like i have  one more example in the math kinase  pathway my kindness which is  pretty important for cancer uh  occurring occurrence is also affected by  uh  microgravity so that's one part which is  highly studied which is pretty much  established but  now we are studying under a different  condition and many different things  might unfold  so therefore that would be also  something interesting to look into  and finally i would suggest our this is  kind of my hypothesis that what can be  other ways in which microgravity affects  other than epigenetics  well there were two studies which really  made me think like this so the two ways  which i think is changes in protein  folding patterns  and that can cause some sort of  conformational changes and stuffs  and there could be changes in the cell  signaling pathways all together  and that was fostered because of these  two  experiments one was conducted by mark in  association with nasa so this is ketuta  is  one uh monoclonal antibody that's again  a protein  and you can see that it's formation and  it's  structure and the way it behaves  completely changes under microgravity as  compared to  normal gravity on earth so on earth it  really doesn't form  a uniform pattern it's chaotic whereas  under microgravity they  start forming a beautiful shape so  protein folding or  shape conformational shapes might be  affected by  microgravity that of proteins because  this is a monoclonal antibody  maybe this is one aspect this is a  kind of hidden experiment of mark so i  don't really have any associated paper  which  talks in detail about this and this is  again  the analysis of cancer cell lines under  microgravity so  when microgravity ceases to exist the  cell lines start rotating and once they  rotate they freely form 3d organoids  which are known as multicellular  spheroids so  without the application of any other  factor these  cell lines start to form this 3d  organized or speed up structure  but what's more interesting is they lose  their cell signaling  capabilities and once they lose the cell  signaling capabilities  the metastasis actually comes to a halt  metastasis has been seen to be stopped  because of  the intervention of microgravity so  again that kind of supports the idea  that there are certain cell signaling  changes which comes  into picture when microgravity hits them  all right so that's all i had for today  a really short  accumulation of my studies and ideas  and i would love to hear from you guys  what you have  to say about it

